<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vasily II of Moscow</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1415">1415</a>–<a href="/years/1462">1462</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Vasily the Blind</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vasily II was grand prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> from 1425 to 1462. His reign was dominated by a long civil war with his uncle Yuri of Galich and Yuri's sons; in 1446 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dmitry-shemyaka">Dmitry Shemyaka</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-andreyevich-of-mozhaysk">Ivan of Mozhaysk</a> captured and blinded him. He recovered the throne, annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanages</a> such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozhaysk">Mozhaysk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serpukhov">Serpukhov</a>, and left a strengthened Moscow to his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iii">Ivan III</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vasily-ii-of-moscow.jpg" alt="Vasily II of Moscow" width="320" height="425" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: collective · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Titulyarnik_-_Vasily_Vasiliyevich.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Vasily II Vasilyevich was born on 10 March 1415, the youngest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasily-i-of-moscow">Vasily I of Moscow</a> and Sophia of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, the only daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vytautas-the-great">Vytautas the Great</a>. He was the only son to survive his father, since his elder brother Ivan died in 1417. At his father's death he was proclaimed grand prince at the age of ten, with his mother as regent. His uncle Yuri of Zvenigorod, prince of Galich-Mersky, and Yuri's sons <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasily-kosoy">Vasily Kosoy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dmitry-shemyaka">Dmitry Shemyaka</a> took the chance to press their own claims, which rested on the testament of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dmitry-donskoy">Dmitry Donskoy</a>, Yuri's father. Vytautas supported his grandson.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After Vytautas died in 1430, Yuri went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> and returned with a license to take the throne, but the khan did not support him further. In 1433 Yuri attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> and captured Vasily, who had been betrayed by the boyar Ivan Vsevolzhsky. Yuri pardoned him and sent him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolomna">Kolomna</a>, where Vasily gathered malcontents; Yuri left Moscow. After Yuri died in 1434, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasily-kosoy">Vasily Kosoy</a> took the Kremlin until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dmitry-shemyaka">Dmitry Shemyaka</a> allied with Vasily II and drove his brother out in 1435; Vasily Kosoy was captured and blinded.</p><p>In 1439 Vasily II fled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulugh-muhammad">Ulugh Muhammad</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a> besieged it. Six years later he led an army against Ulugh Muhammad, was defeated and captured, and was released after about five months for an enormous ransom. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dmitry-shemyaka">Dmitry Shemyaka</a> took control of Moscow and in 1446 had Vasily blinded and exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uglich">Uglich</a>, which gave him the nickname the Blind. Vasily soon gathered supporters and regained the throne. In the 1450s he captured Galich-Mersky and poisoned Dmitry, ending the struggle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The victory settled the principle of succession from father to son against collateral succession, a major cause of medieval feuds, and made centralisation possible. Vasily eliminated almost all the small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanages</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> principality, and his campaigns of 1441 to 1460 increased Moscow's hold over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzdal">Suzdal</a>, the Vyatka lands, Novgorod and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pskov">Pskov</a>. In 1448 a council of Russian bishops elected Jonah as metropolitan, tantamount to independence from the patriarch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. Vasily took the title sovereign of all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, and made his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iii">Ivan III</a> co-ruler from the late 1450s. He died on 27 March 1462, and Ivan III succeeded him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vasily II of Moscow” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The principle was tested in the civil war of 1425–1453, when <b>Vasily II</b> fought his uncle Yury of Zvenigorod and his cousins Vasily Kosoy and Dmitry Shemyaka; Vasily was captured by the Tatars of Ulugh Muhammad in 1445 and blinded by his rivals in 1446, yet regained the…</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Novgorod’s militia proved no match for Moscow’s armies: <b>Vasily II</b> defeated it at Staraya Russa in 1456, and Ivan III’s commanders routed it on the Shelon River in 1471.</blockquote><a href="/empire/novgorod-republic">Novgorod Republic · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>It was later granted to Donskoy's son Yuri, whose line fought <b>Vasily II</b> for the throne of Moscow in the civil war of the 1420s to 1450s.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-galich-mersky">Principality of Galich-Mersky</a></li><li><blockquote>During the long civil war over the Moscow throne, Andrei's son Ivan of Mozhaysk joined Dmitry Shemyaka, and in 1446 he helped seize <b>Vasily II</b> at the Trinity Monastery, after which Vasily was blinded.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-mozhaysk">Principality of Mozhaysk</a></li><li><blockquote>During Moscow's civil war Vladimir's grandson Vasily Yaroslavich supported <b>Vasily II</b>, but in 1456 the grand prince had him arrested and annexed Serpukhov.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-serpukhov">Principality of Serpukhov</a></li><li><blockquote>Unlike his brother Ivan of Mozhaysk, Mikhail stayed loyal to <b>Vasily II</b> during Moscow's civil war, and he was allowed to keep his lands after Mozhaysk was taken in 1454.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-vereya">Principality of Vereya</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.75,37.62&amp;year=1445" aria-label="Moscow on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5370 263 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Vasily II of Moscow"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5820 533h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5820 533h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.75,37.62&amp;year=1445">What was here in 1445? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
